I’ve localized the mismatch to the fact that Lighthouse only overrides the network‐level User‐Agent header via CDP but never updates `window.navigator.userAgent` in the page itself. That means when Lighthouse later does:

  driver.getUserAgent()  // → evaluates `navigator.userAgent` in the page

it sees the original DevTools UA (e.g. CrOS/Linux) instead of the emulated mobile UA string.

The fix is to also inject a script at document‐start to redefine `navigator.userAgent` to the same string used in `Network.setUserAgentOverride`. That way both the header and the JS API line up, and runtimeSettings.userAgent will now match the actual UA sent on the wire.

Here’s a single *SEARCH/REPLACE* patch against `lighthouse-core/lib/emulation.js`:

```javascript
### lighthouse-core/lib/emulation.js
<<<<<<< SEARCH
